ECMA-376 (Office Open XML) Transitional XML Schemas — vendored copy
==================================================================

WHAT THIS IS
------------
The XSD schemas that the L2 artifact gate (scripts/office-skills-selftest.py, check
D2) validates generated .docx/.xlsx parts against. They are a DEVELOPMENT-TIME
dependency of the gate: they are not shipped in the application, are not part of
skills-builtin.zip, and no runtime code reads them.


PROVENANCE
----------
Source   ECMA-376 Part 4, 5th edition (December 2016), "Office Open XML File
         Formats — Transitional Migration Features"
         https://ecma-international.org/publications-and-standards/standards/ecma-376/
File     ECMA-376-4_5th_edition_december_2016.zip
         sha256 prefix bd25da1109f73762356596918bf5ff8b… (downloaded 2026-08-02)
Member   OfficeOpenXML-XMLSchema-Transitional.zip  (26 .xsd files)

WHY PART 4 AND NOT PART 1. Part 1 is "the" specification and is the natural thing to
reach for, but it ships the STRICT schemas, whose targetNamespace is
`purl.oclc.org/ooxml/...`. Every document Word and Excel actually produce — and every
fixture in this repository — is TRANSITIONAL, `schemas.openxmlformats.org/...`.
Validating real documents against the Strict schemas fails at the namespace level for
all of them, which reads as "our output is non-conformant" when nothing is wrong with
it. Verified 2026-08-02 by comparing targetNamespace against this repo's own
fixtures.


LICENCE
-------
ECMA-376 carries NO copyright notice and NO licence text. This was verified rather
than assumed: a full-text scan of Part 1 (5,039 pages) and Part 4 (1,553 pages) finds
no Ecma copyright statement anywhere — the only "Copyright" strings in Part 1 are W3C
notices inside example markup — and neither XSD package contains any file that is not
a .xsd. Ecma's general text-copyright policy applies only to documents that carry its
notice, which was introduced in 2009 and not applied retrospectively; ECMA-376
originates in 2006.

The operative statement is therefore Ecma's by-laws, article 9.4
(https://ecma-international.org/policies/by-laws/):

    "All documents when approved shall be made available to all interested parties
     without restriction."

The same reasoning is what the Apache Software Foundation relies on to ship XmlBeans
generated from these schemas inside Apache POI (poi-ooxml-full) under Apache-2.0 —
see that artifact's NOTICE, which cites article 9.4 together with the patent
declarations Microsoft and Adobe filed with Ecma, and which Oracle among others
redistributes commercially.

Microsoft's Open Specification Promise and the Microsoft and Adobe patent
declarations to Ecma cover the patent side.

This is a record of the basis for including these files. It is not legal advice.


MODIFICATIONS
-------------
Two files are modified from the upstream package. Both changes are recorded here so
the copy is not silently divergent:

  wml.xsd, shared-math.xsd
      `<xsd:import namespace="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"/>`
    → `<xsd:import namespace="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"
                   schemaLocation="xml.xsd"/>`

    As shipped, these imports name the XML namespace but give no schemaLocation, and
    the package contains no xml.xsd. The schema therefore fails to LOAD at all:
    "The QName value '{http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace}space' does not resolve
    to a(n) attribute declaration". This is a defect in the upstream distribution,
    not a change to the schema's meaning.

No other file is altered.


